IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Telecommunications (Consumer Protection and Service Standards) Act 1999 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- what the phone
-- customer meets: the universal service obligation to supply a standard telephone service on request (ss 6, 9, 9E, 12C,
-- 12D); untimed local calls (Part 3, ss 104-106, 108-110); the customer service guarantee: damages for a breach of a
-- performance standard, the time to sue, the provider's time to decide and pay, and waiver (Part 5, ss 115(2), 116, 117(3),
-- 117A, 120, 120A); and the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman scheme (Part 6, ss 127, 128, 129).
--
-- SEAT: the phone customer -- a residential customer, a small business, or a charity, and an end-user of a carriage service.
-- "Am I entitled to a phone line? Who must supply it, and with what equipment? Must my provider offer untimed local calls?
-- My connection or repair was late: what can I get, and by when? Must my provider belong to the Ombudsman scheme, and can
-- the Ombudsman take my complaint?" Provisions addressed to the Minister, the ACMA or the provider are encoded as what they
-- mean for the customer.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 45, compilation date 31 October 2025 (includes amendments up to Act No. 50, 2025), as deposited
-- at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A00441.txt.
--
-- INSTRUMENTS NOT DEPOSITED. The customer service guarantee standard and its scale of damages (ss 115, 117), the Minister's
-- USO performance standards and benchmarks (ss 12EB-12EE), the s 9(2D) and s 9(3) determinations, any s 8J declaration,
-- any s 12A determination, the s 147 emergency call services determination, and the regulations under ss 6(1)(c) and 9E.
-- Each is asked as a fact where a question needs it; NOTES.md says which.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Part 2 beyond ss 9, 9E, 12C-12D (contracts and grants, the levy, the payphone rules), Part 3 s 107 (the
-- regulations' scheme for customers outside standard zones), Part 4 (the Regional Broadband Scheme), Part 5 Division 3
-- (wholesale standards), ss 117B-117C, 118, 118A, 119, Part 8 (emergency call services: the duties are in the ACMA's
-- determination), Part 9 (price control), Part 9B (regional reviews). Broadband: the statutory infrastructure provider
-- regime is in Part 19 of the Telecommunications Act 1997, not this Act (NOTES.md finding 1).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the customer's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Telecommunications (Consumer Protection and Service Standards) Act 1999 -- the phone customer`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- s 117(3)
    `the scale of damages may not specify more than $25,000 for a category (s 117(3)): an amount above that is not a valid scale amount`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `The universal service obligation -- ss 6, 9, 9E, 12C, 12D`

-- s 9(1)(a): "to ensure that standard telephone services are reasonably accessible to all people in Australia on an
-- equitable basis, wherever they reside or carry on business"; (2): "includes the obligation to supply standard telephone
-- services to people in Australia on request". It does not arise: (2AAA) after a s 8J declaration (satisfactory
-- alternative contractual arrangements) survives disallowance; (2C) where the request does not comply with the requirements
-- of a determination under (2D); (2E) in the circumstances specified in a determination under (3). Those instruments are
-- asked as facts. s 12C(1): the obligation for an area is imposed on its primary universal service provider; s 12D(1): Telstra
-- Limited is taken to be that provider except in a "special determined area".
DECLARE `My standard telephone service` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the primary universal service provider for my area must supply me a standard telephone service on request (s 9(1)(a), (2), s 12C)`
    `no: a request is needed; the obligation is to supply on request (s 9(2))`
    `no: the obligation is to people in Australia, where they reside or carry on business (s 9(1)(a))`
    `no: my request does not comply with the requirements the Minister has determined under s 9(2D) (s 9(2C))`
    `no: my circumstances are ones the Minister has determined under s 9(3) in which the obligation does not arise (s 9(2E))`
    `no: the Minister's s 8J declaration that there are satisfactory alternative contractual arrangements has survived disallowance (s 9(2AAA))`

@export Am I entitled to have a standard telephone service (a fixed phone line) supplied to me under the universal service obligation?
GIVEN `I live or carry on business in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you live, or carry on business, in Australia at the place where you want the service
      `I have asked for the service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have requested a standard telephone service
      `my request meets the Minister's requirements for requests` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your request complies with the requirements (if any) the Minister has set for requests by legislative instrument under s 9(2D); answer yes if none are set
      `the Minister has excluded my circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your circumstances are ones the Minister has specified, by legislative instrument under s 9(3), in which the obligation does not arise
      `a section 8J declaration has taken effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Minister has declared under s 8J that there are satisfactory alternative contractual arrangements for standard telephone services, and the declaration was not disallowed
GIVETH A `My standard telephone service`
`Am I entitled to a standard telephone service` MEANS
    IF `a section 8J declaration has taken effect`
    THEN `no: the Minister's s 8J declaration that there are satisfactory alternative contractual arrangements has survived disallowance (s 9(2AAA))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `I live or carry on business in Australia`)
         THEN `no: the obligation is to people in Australia, where they reside or carry on business (s 9(1)(a))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `I have asked for the service`)
              THEN `no: a request is needed; the obligation is to supply on request (s 9(2))`
              ELSE IF (NOT `my request meets the Minister's requirements for requests`)
                   THEN `no: my request does not comply with the requirements the Minister has determined under s 9(2D) (s 9(2C))`
                   ELSE IF `the Minister has excluded my circumstances`
                        THEN `no: my circumstances are ones the Minister has determined under s 9(3) in which the obligation does not arise (s 9(2E))`
                        ELSE `yes: the primary universal service provider for my area must supply me a standard telephone service on request (s 9(1)(a), (2), s 12C)`

DECLARE `Who must supply it` IS ONE OF
    -- s 12D(1)
    `Telstra Limited, which is taken to be the primary universal service provider outside a special determined area (s 12D(1))`
    -- s 12A(1), s 12C(1)
    `the one provider the Minister has determined for my area (ss 12A(1), 12C(1))`
    -- s 12A(2A), s 12C(2): imposed on each, discharged by any
    `each of the providers the Minister has determined for my area; any one of them may supply it (ss 12A(2A), 12C(2))`

@export Which provider must supply my standard telephone service under the universal service obligation?
GIVEN `the Minister has determined providers for my area` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Minister has determined, under s 12A, a provider other than Telstra (or two or more providers) as primary universal service provider for your area or for all of Australia
      `more than one provider is determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that determination names two or more providers
GIVETH A `Who must supply it`
`Who must supply my standard telephone service` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the Minister has determined providers for my area`)
    THEN `Telstra Limited, which is taken to be the primary universal service provider outside a special determined area (s 12D(1))`
    ELSE IF `more than one provider is determined`
         THEN `each of the providers the Minister has determined for my area; any one of them may supply it (ss 12A(2A), 12C(2))`
         ELSE `the one provider the Minister has determined for my area (ss 12A(1), 12C(1))`

-- s 6(1)(a)-(b): a standard telephone service is a carriage service for voice telephony, or, where voice telephony is not
-- practical for an end-user with a disability and another equivalent form of communication would be required by the
-- Disability Discrimination Act 1992, for that form. s 9E(1)(a)-(b): its supply includes the customer equipment the
-- regulations prescribe or, if none, "a telephone handset that does not have switching functions", or other equipment
-- the Disability Discrimination Act requires instead. s 9(4): no obligation for equipment the customer asks not to be
-- supplied with. s 9(6): "requires the customer concerned to be given the option of hiring the equipment".
DECLARE `My equipment` IS ONE OF
    `none: I asked not to be supplied with it, so the obligation does not extend to it (s 9(4))`
    `the equipment the Disability Discrimination Act 1992 requires instead of a handset (for example a text telephone), with the option of hiring it (ss 6(1)(b), 9E(1), 9(6))`
    `the customer equipment the regulations prescribe, with the option of hiring it (ss 9E(1)(a), 9(6))`
    `a telephone handset without switching functions, with the option of hiring it (ss 9E(1)(b), 9(6))`

@export What equipment must come with my standard telephone service under the universal service obligation?
GIVEN `I asked not to be supplied with equipment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the provider not to supply you with the phone or other equipment
      `I need other equipment because of a disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of a disability, voice telephony is not practical for you and other equipment (such as a text telephone) would have to be supplied to comply with the Disability Discrimination Act 1992
      `the regulations prescribe the equipment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether regulations under s 9E(1)(a) prescribe the customer equipment to be supplied
GIVETH A `My equipment`
`What equipment must come with my service` MEANS
    IF `I asked not to be supplied with equipment`
    THEN `none: I asked not to be supplied with it, so the obligation does not extend to it (s 9(4))`
    ELSE IF `I need other equipment because of a disability`
         THEN `the equipment the Disability Discrimination Act 1992 requires instead of a handset (for example a text telephone), with the option of hiring it (ss 6(1)(b), 9E(1), 9(6))`
         ELSE IF `the regulations prescribe the equipment`
              THEN `the customer equipment the regulations prescribe, with the option of hiring it (ss 9E(1)(a), 9(6))`
              ELSE `a telephone handset without switching functions, with the option of hiring it (ss 9E(1)(b), 9(6))`

§§ `Untimed local calls -- Part 3, ss 104-106, 108-110`

-- s 110: a customer in a standard zone is an "eligible customer". s 106(1) (residential customer or registered charity) and
-- (2) (any other customer, for whom the call must also be "a voice call or, if a voice call is not practical for a particular
-- end-user with a disability, a call that is equivalent to a voice call"): a call is an eligible local call if it is
-- "made between points in the applicable zone" and is either "of a kind that, immediately before 20 September 1996, a
-- general carrier offered to supply, or supplied, on an untimed basis between points in that standard zone" or made using
-- a standard telephone service supplied for a universal service purpose, "unless the call involves the use, by at least one
-- party to the call, of a public mobile telecommunications service, or a satellite service, that is not supplied to that
-- party for a universal service purpose". s 104: a provider that charges an eligible customer for eligible local calls on a
-- standard telephone service "must give the customer an untimed local call option".
GIVEN `in a standard zone` IS A BOOLEAN
      `residential or charity` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a voice call` IS A BOOLEAN
      `within the applicable zone` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an old untimed kind, or a universal service` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a mobile or satellite party outside the universal service` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the call is an eligible local call` IF
        `in a standard zone`
    AND (`residential or charity` OR `a voice call`)
    AND `within the applicable zone`
    AND `an old untimed kind, or a universal service`
    AND (NOT `a mobile or satellite party outside the universal service`)

@export Must my phone provider offer to charge my local calls untimed (a flat charge per call, however long)?
GIVEN `I am in a standard zone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your service is in a standard zone: an area in which, before 1 July 1991, Telecom charged calls within the area on an untimed basis
      `I am a residential customer or a registered charity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a residential customer, or a customer that is a registered charity (rather than a business)
      `the calls are voice calls` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the calls are voice calls (or, where voice is not practical because of a disability, calls equivalent to voice calls)
      `the calls are within my local zone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the calls are between points in your applicable zone (your standard zone, or the local area your provider has nominated to the ACMA and you have adopted)
      `the calls are a kind charged untimed in 1996, or my service is a universal service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the calls are of a kind a general carrier supplied untimed in your zone immediately before 20 September 1996, or your phone service is supplied under the universal service obligation (or a s 14 contract or grant)
      `a mobile or satellite service outside the universal service is used` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether either party to the calls uses a mobile phone or satellite service that is not supplied to them under the universal service obligation
      `my provider charges me for these calls` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your provider charges you for the calls using your standard telephone service
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my provider offer untimed local calls` IF
        `my provider charges me for these calls`
    AND `the call is an eligible local call` `I am in a standard zone` `I am a residential customer or a registered charity` `the calls are voice calls` `the calls are within my local zone` `the calls are a kind charged untimed in 1996, or my service is a universal service` `a mobile or satellite service outside the universal service is used`

-- s 105(1): (a) the customer may choose untimed charging on connection; (b) if so, the provider "must not work out the
-- charges for such calls in any other way except with the customer's written consent"; (c) a customer who did not choose,
-- or consented otherwise, "may later give the carriage service provider a written notice"; (d) then the provider must charge
-- untimed "in respect of the earliest practicable period beginning after it receives the notice", and not otherwise without
-- written consent.
DECLARE `How my local calls may be charged` IS ONE OF
    `untimed only: my provider may not charge them any other way without my written consent (s 105(1)(b), (d)(ii))`
    `untimed from the earliest practicable period after my provider receives my notice, and not otherwise without my written consent (s 105(1)(d))`
    `my provider may charge them another way, but I may give written notice at any time that I want them untimed (s 105(1)(c))`

@export My local calls are eligible for the untimed option: may my provider charge them on a timed basis?
GIVEN `I chose untimed charging when connected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you chose, when the service was connected, to have eligible local calls charged untimed
      `I later gave written notice asking for untimed charging` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you later gave your provider written notice that you want those calls charged untimed
      `I have since consented in writing to another basis` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after you last chose or asked for untimed charging, you gave written consent to the calls being charged another way
GIVETH A `How my local calls may be charged`
`How may my local calls be charged` MEANS
    IF `I have since consented in writing to another basis`
    THEN `my provider may charge them another way, but I may give written notice at any time that I want them untimed (s 105(1)(c))`
    ELSE IF `I later gave written notice asking for untimed charging`
         THEN `untimed from the earliest practicable period after my provider receives my notice, and not otherwise without my written consent (s 105(1)(d))`
         ELSE IF `I chose untimed charging when connected`
              THEN `untimed only: my provider may not charge them any other way without my written consent (s 105(1)(b), (d)(ii))`
              ELSE `my provider may charge them another way, but I may give written notice at any time that I want them untimed (s 105(1)(c))`

§§ `The customer service guarantee -- Part 5, ss 115(2), 116, 117, 117A, 120, 120A (when a connection, repair or appointment went wrong)`

-- s 116(1): "If (a) a carriage service provider contravenes a standard in force under section 115; and (b) the contravention
-- relates to a particular customer; the carriage service provider is liable to pay damages to the customer". s 114:
-- "customer includes prospective customer". s 115(2): a standard about a kind of service applies at a location only where
-- the provider supplies or offers to supply that kind there; (2A) not to a wholesale service. s 120(2): to the extent of a
-- waiver, the provider "is not bound by, and need not comply with," the standards; (7) no waiver for a standard telephone
-- service supplied under the universal service obligation (or a s 14 contract or grant). The standard itself (what the
-- periods are) is the ACMA's instrument, not deposited: whether it was breached is asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Am I owed damages under the guarantee` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my provider is liable to pay me damages for the contravention (s 116(1))`
    `no: the standard does not apply to my provider at my location, because it does not supply or offer that kind of service there (s 115(2))`
    `no: I validly waived the customer service guarantee for this service (s 120(2))`
    `no: my provider has not contravened a performance standard in relation to me`

@export Is my phone or internet provider liable to pay me damages because it missed the customer service guarantee (a late connection, a late repair, a missed appointment)?
GIVEN `my provider supplies or offers that kind of service at my location` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your provider supplies, or offers to supply, that kind of service at your location
      `my provider broke a customer service standard in my case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your provider contravened a performance standard made by the ACMA under s 115 (for example, missed the connection or repair period, or an appointment), and the contravention relates to you
      `I signed a waiver of the guarantee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you waived the customer service guarantee for this service, in the form and under the rules the ACMA sets
      `my service is a universal service phone line` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service is a standard telephone service supplied under the universal service obligation (or under a s 14 contract or grant for that purpose)
GIVETH A `Am I owed damages under the guarantee`
`Am I owed damages under the customer service guarantee` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my provider supplies or offers that kind of service at my location`)
    THEN `no: the standard does not apply to my provider at my location, because it does not supply or offer that kind of service there (s 115(2))`
    ELSE IF `I signed a waiver of the guarantee` AND (NOT `my service is a universal service phone line`)
         THEN `no: I validly waived the customer service guarantee for this service (s 120(2))`
         ELSE IF `my provider broke a customer service standard in my case`
              THEN `yes: my provider is liable to pay me damages for the contravention (s 116(1))`
              ELSE `no: my provider has not contravened a performance standard in relation to me`

-- s 116(2): "equal to the relevant amount specified in the scale in force under section 117"; (3): reduced "(but not below
-- zero) by the amount of the credit or payment" the provider made under another right or remedy arising out of the same
-- event. s 117(3): a scale amount "must not exceed $25,000". The scale amount is the ACMA's instrument, asked as a fact.
@export How much must my provider pay me in customer service guarantee damages?
GIVEN `the amount the scale of damages sets` IS A NUMBER @desc The dollar amount the ACMA's scale of damages specifies for the category of contravention (for example, the amount for each working day of delay, times the days)
      `what my provider already credited or paid me for the same event` IS A NUMBER @desc What your provider has already credited to your account or paid you, under another right or remedy, for the same event; 0 if nothing
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
`How much guarantee damages must my provider pay` MEANS
    IF `the amount the scale of damages sets` GREATER THAN 25000
    THEN `not covered` `the scale of damages may not specify more than $25,000 for a category (s 117(3)): an amount above that is not a valid scale amount`
    ELSE IF `what my provider already credited or paid me for the same event` GREATER THAN `the amount the scale of damages sets`
         THEN `answered` 0
         ELSE `answered` (`the amount the scale of damages sets` MINUS `what my provider already credited or paid me for the same event`)

-- s 116(6): "within 2 years after: (a) in the case of a contravention that continued throughout a period--the time when the
-- contravention began; or (b) in any other case--the time when the contravention occurred."
@export What is the last day on which I can sue my provider for customer service guarantee damages?
GIVEN `the day the contravention began or occurred` IS A DATE @desc The day the contravention occurred or, if it continued (for example, a repair that stayed late), the day it began
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day to sue for guarantee damages` MEANS `add years` `the day the contravention began or occurred` 2

-- s 117A(1): "within 14 days after that time, the carriage service provider must decide whether to accept that liability";
-- (3): if not, "written notification of the decision within 14 weeks after the decision is made"; (4)-(5): if accepted, the
-- liability "must be discharged within the period of 14 weeks after the decision is made" (a credit, in time for the first
-- or second bill in that period where practicable).
@export By when must my provider decide whether it accepts that it owes me guarantee damages?
GIVEN `the day my provider first had reason to believe it might owe me` IS A DATE @desc The day your provider first had reason to believe that an event had occurred that is reasonably likely to make it liable to you (for example, the day the connection or repair period was missed)
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day for my provider to decide on liability` MEANS `the day my provider first had reason to believe it might owe me` PLUS 14

@export By when must my provider pay or credit my guarantee damages, or tell me in writing that it will not?
GIVEN `the day my provider decided` IS A DATE @desc The day your provider decided whether to accept the liability
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day for my provider to pay or tell me` MEANS `the day my provider decided` PLUS 98

-- s 120(1)-(5): a waiver made under the ACMA's instrument, in its form, with a statement summarising its consequences; (6)
-- not in the provider's standard form of agreement; (7) never for a standard telephone service supplied under the universal
-- service obligation (or a s 14 contract or grant). s 120A: a provider may supply a service on condition of a waiver.
DECLARE `Can I be asked to waive the guarantee` IS ONE OF
    `no: I cannot waive my protection and rights under Part 5 for a universal service phone line (s 120(7))`
    `yes: my provider may make supply conditional on a waiver (s 120A), but the waiver must follow the ACMA's rules and form, with a statement of its consequences, and cannot be in the provider's standard form of agreement (s 120(3)-(6))`

@export Can my provider make me waive the customer service guarantee?
GIVEN `my service is a universal service phone line` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service is a standard telephone service supplied under the universal service obligation (or under a s 14 contract or grant for that purpose)
GIVETH A `Can I be asked to waive the guarantee`
`Can my provider make me waive the guarantee` MEANS
    IF `my service is a universal service phone line`
    THEN `no: I cannot waive my protection and rights under Part 5 for a universal service phone line (s 120(7))`
    ELSE `yes: my provider may make supply conditional on a waiver (s 120A), but the waiver must follow the ACMA's rules and form, with a statement of its consequences, and cannot be in the provider's standard form of agreement (s 120(3)-(6))`

§§ `The Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman -- Part 6, ss 127, 128, 129`

-- s 128(1): "Each carrier and each eligible carriage service provider must ... enter into" the Telecommunications Industry
-- Ombudsman scheme. s 127: an eligible carriage service provider supplies "(i) a standard telephone service, where any of
-- the customers are residential customers or small business customers; or (ii) a public mobile telecommunications service;
-- or (iii) a carriage service that enables end-users to access the internet", or is an intermediary arranging one. s 129:
-- the ACMA may exempt; s 130, s 131: the ACMA may direct a provider, or a class, to join.
DECLARE `Must my provider be in the Ombudsman scheme` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is a carrier, and must enter into the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman scheme (s 128(1))`
    `yes: it is an eligible carriage service provider, and must enter into the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman scheme (ss 127, 128(1))`
    `yes: the ACMA has directed it, or its class, to enter into the scheme (ss 130, 131)`
    `no: the ACMA has declared it exempt (s 129)`
    `no: the Act does not require it to join, though membership must be open to it (s 128(7))`

@export Must my phone, mobile or internet provider be a member of the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman scheme?
GIVEN `my provider is a carrier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your provider holds a carrier licence (owns the network units used)
      `it supplies a fixed phone service to homes or small businesses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it supplies, or arranges, a standard telephone service where any of the customers are residential or small business customers
      `it supplies a mobile phone service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it supplies, or arranges, a public mobile telecommunications service
      `it supplies internet access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it supplies, or arranges, a carriage service that lets end-users access the internet
      `the ACMA has directed it to join` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ACMA has directed your provider, or a class it belongs to, to enter into the scheme
      `the ACMA has exempted it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ACMA has declared your provider (or its class) exempt from having to join
GIVETH A `Must my provider be in the Ombudsman scheme`
`Must my provider be in the Ombudsman scheme` MEANS
    IF `the ACMA has directed it to join`
    THEN `yes: the ACMA has directed it, or its class, to enter into the scheme (ss 130, 131)`
    ELSE IF `the ACMA has exempted it`
         THEN `no: the ACMA has declared it exempt (s 129)`
         ELSE IF `my provider is a carrier`
              THEN `yes: it is a carrier, and must enter into the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman scheme (s 128(1))`
              ELSE IF `it supplies a fixed phone service to homes or small businesses` OR `it supplies a mobile phone service` OR `it supplies internet access`
                   THEN `yes: it is an eligible carriage service provider, and must enter into the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman scheme (ss 127, 128(1))`
                   ELSE `no: the Act does not require it to join, though membership must be open to it (s 128(7))`

-- s 128(4): the scheme "must provide for the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman to (a) investigate; and (b) make
-- determinations relating to; and (c) give directions relating to; complaints about carriage services by end-users";
-- (5) "a complaint about billing, or the manner of charging". (5A): the scheme "may also provide" for complaints about the
-- connection of premises by a statutory infrastructure provider. (6): "must not provide for the Telecommunications Industry
-- Ombudsman to investigate complaints about: (a) the levels at which tariffs charged for the supply of carriage services are
-- set; or (b) the content of a content service." (4A): the end-user "is not liable to pay any fee or charge (however
-- described) to the provider of the carriage service in respect of a complaint".
DECLARE `What my complaint is about` IS ONE OF
    `billing, or the manner of charging`
    `the service itself (a fault, a connection, the supply)`
    `the level at which the provider's prices are set`
    `the content of a content service`
    `the connection of my premises by a statutory infrastructure provider`

DECLARE `Can the Ombudsman take my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the scheme must provide for the Ombudsman to investigate, determine and give directions on it, and my provider may not charge me for my complaint (s 128(4), (4A), (5))`
    `perhaps: the scheme may provide for the Ombudsman to deal with it, but the Act does not require it (s 128(5A))`
    `no: the scheme must not provide for the Ombudsman to investigate it (s 128(6))`

@export Can the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman take my complaint about my provider?
GIVEN `what my complaint is about` IS A `What my complaint is about` @desc What the complaint you want to make, as an end-user of the service, is about
GIVETH A `Can the Ombudsman take my complaint`
`Can the Ombudsman take my complaint` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what my complaint is about`
    WHEN `billing, or the manner of charging` THEN `yes: the scheme must provide for the Ombudsman to investigate, determine and give directions on it, and my provider may not charge me for my complaint (s 128(4), (4A), (5))`
    WHEN `the service itself (a fault, a connection, the supply)` THEN `yes: the scheme must provide for the Ombudsman to investigate, determine and give directions on it, and my provider may not charge me for my complaint (s 128(4), (4A), (5))`
    WHEN `the connection of my premises by a statutory infrastructure provider` THEN `perhaps: the scheme may provide for the Ombudsman to deal with it, but the Act does not require it (s 128(5A))`
    OTHERWISE `no: the scheme must not provide for the Ombudsman to investigate it (s 128(6))`
